Overview
This simulator provides a comprehensive platform for researching autonomous satellite constellation operations with fault tolerance capabilities:

Multi-Satellite Constellation Management: Configure and manage satellite clusters with leader-follower architectures
Realistic Fault Injection: Simulate hardware failures including encoder drift, mechanical friction, power limitations, and battery degradation
ML-Based Fault Detection: Real-time anomaly detection using trained autoencoder models
DRL Task Reassignment: Intelligent task redistribution using Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)
Advanced Visualization: Generate Unity-compatible binary files for 3D visualization in Vizard
Data Analysis: Comprehensive plotting and telemetry logging for performance evaluation
Mission Planning: Test fault tolerance and recovery strategies for satellite constellations


Key Features
Constellation Management

Support for multiple orbital configurations (LEO, MEO, GEO)
Up to 4 clusters with 2-6 satellites each
Leader-follower hierarchical structure
Formation patterns: Line, Triangle, Diamond, Leader-Follower
Quick-setup presets for common constellation patterns
Individual satellite parameter customization
Real-time orbital propagation using Basilisk dynamics

Fault Simulation

Friction Fault: Simulates bearing degradation and mechanical wear
Power Limit Fault: Models power system constraints and failures
Encoder Fault: Introduces measurement errors in reaction wheel feedback
Battery Fault: Simulates battery degradation and increased power consumption
Cluster-wide fault injection capabilities
Periodic fault patterns
Real-time fault injection during simulation

Fault Detection & Recovery

ML-based real-time fault detection using autoencoder models
XAI model explanations (LIME, SHAP)
Multi-algorithm detection (ML, Statistical, Trend-based)
Automatic fault flagging with confidence scores
DRL-powered task reassignment system
Safe mode fallback mechanism

Analysis Tools

Real-time plotting of spacecraft dynamics
Attitude error analysis
Reaction wheel performance monitoring
Power consumption tracking
Inter-satellite distance calculations
Cluster communication analysis
Formation quality metrics
Fault detection performance plots
DRL decision visualization and analysis

Visualization

Vizard-compatible binary file generation
Configurable camera views for each satellite
Ground target tracking visualization
Orbit trajectory display
Cluster-specific color coding
Formation geometry visualization
Target coverage cone visualization
Time-compressed playback (30-minute simulation in 30-second playback)


System Requirements
Prerequisites

Python: 3.10 or higher
Basilisk: Latest version properly installed
Operating System: Windows 10/11, macOS, or Linux
RAM: Minimum 8GB (16GB recommended for large constellations)
Storage: At least 5GB free space for simulation outputs and ML models

Python Dependencies
txtnumpy>=1.19.0
matplotlib>=3.3.0
tkinter (usually included with Python)
Pillow>=8.0.0
tensorflow>=2.8.0       # For ML fault detection
scikit-learn>=0.24.0    # For data preprocessing
pandas>=1.3.0           # For data analysis
stable-baselines3>=1.5.0 # For DRL training (optional)
shap>=0.40.0           # For XAI explanations (optional)
lime>=0.2.0            # For XAI explanations (optional)

Installation
1. Clone the Repository
bashgit clone https://github.com/yourusername/satelliteConstellation-basilisk.git
cd satelliteConstellation-basilisk/ProjectWork/BskSim
2. Verify Basilisk Installation
bashpython -c "from Basilisk import __path__; print('Basilisk found at:', __path__[0])"
3. Install Dependencies
bash# Create virtual environment (recommended)
python -m venv bskrl-env

# Activate environment
# Windows:
bskrl-env\Scripts\activate
# Linux/Mac:
source bskrl-env/bin/activate

# Install dependencies
pip install numpy matplotlib Pillow tensorflow scikit-learn pandas

# Optional: Install DRL training libraries
pip install stable-baselines3

# Optional: Install XAI libraries
pip install shap lime
4. Verify ML Models
Ensure the ML models are present:

core/anomaly_detection_model.keras - Fault detection model
core/drl_task_reassignment.h5 - DRL task reassignment model

5. Test Installation
bashcd core
python run_fault_simulator.py
```

